1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,272
This one is a drawing question, worth 10 to 14 points: the prototype

2
00:00:04,372 --> 00:00:08,708
diagram. The points are in the arrows, so let's build the diagram one

3
00:00:08,808 --> 00:00:12,116
arrow at a time and talk about why each one is there.

4
00:00:14,200 --> 00:00:17,752
Here's typical exam code. A constructor function Boat that

5
00:00:17,852 --> 00:00:20,775
stores a name, a capacity property added to Boat

6
00:00:20,875 --> 00:00:23,484
dot prototype, and two boats made with new.

7
00:00:23,566 --> 00:00:26,295
You need to draw the function, its prototype

8
00:00:26,395 --> 00:00:30,216
object, both instances, and every prototype, constructor, and

9
00:00:30,316 --> 00:00:32,466
proto link, all the way up to null.

10
00:00:34,533 --> 00:00:38,139
Start with Boat itself. Functions are objects too, and every

11
00:00:38,239 --> 00:00:42,463
function automatically gets a property called prototype that points to

12
00:00:42,563 --> 00:00:46,232
a separate object, Boat dot prototype. The capacity line puts

13
00:00:46,332 --> 00:00:48,641
capacity on that object, not on a or b.

14
00:00:48,733 --> 00:00:51,987
The prototype object has a constructor property that points

15
00:00:52,087 --> 00:00:54,090
right back to Boat. So you get a pair

16
00:00:54,190 --> 00:00:57,273
of arrows: prototype going out, constructor coming back.

17
00:00:57,366 --> 00:01:00,978
Now the instances. new Boat makes a fresh object and runs

18
00:01:01,078 --> 00:01:04,365
the constructor, so a and b each get their own name.

19
00:01:04,465 --> 00:01:08,077
And new also sets each object's hidden proto link to Boat

20
00:01:08,177 --> 00:01:11,594
dot prototype. That link is how they'll find capacity.

21
00:01:11,666 --> 00:01:15,545
Don't stop there. Boat dot prototype is itself a plain object,

22
00:01:15,645 --> 00:01:19,715
so its proto is Object dot prototype, where methods like toString

23
00:01:19,815 --> 00:01:23,694
live. And Object dot prototype's proto is null. That's the end

24
00:01:23,794 --> 00:01:26,902
of the chain, and the exam expects you to draw it.

25
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:31,735
Exam trap. Don't draw a prototype property or a capacity

26
00:01:31,835 --> 00:01:35,022
inside a or b. Only the function has a prototype

27
00:01:35,122 --> 00:01:38,652
property. Ordinary objects have a proto link instead.

28
00:01:40,733 --> 00:01:44,427
Now, why does this matter? Property lookup. For a dot name,

29
00:01:44,527 --> 00:01:48,865
JavaScript checks a first, finds name right there, and returns local.

30
00:01:50,933 --> 00:01:54,678
For a dot capacity, it's not on a, so JavaScript follows

31
00:01:54,778 --> 00:01:58,249
the proto arrow to Boat dot prototype, and finds 50.

32
00:02:00,333 --> 00:02:03,624
a dot toString isn't on a or on Boat dot prototype,

33
00:02:03,724 --> 00:02:07,481
so it keeps climbing and finds it on Object dot prototype.

34
00:02:09,566 --> 00:02:12,343
And a dot wings walks the whole chain, hits

35
00:02:12,443 --> 00:02:16,090
null, and gives undefined. Not an error, just undefined.

36
00:02:18,766 --> 00:02:22,809
What if we assign a dot capacity equals 10? Assignment never

37
00:02:22,909 --> 00:02:26,399
walks the chain. It creates a new own property on a,

38
00:02:26,499 --> 00:02:30,611
which shadows the prototype's value. So a dot capacity is 10,

39
00:02:30,711 --> 00:02:33,234
but b still finds 50 on the prototype.

40
00:02:33,333 --> 00:02:37,147
And if we change the prototype's capacity to 75, every

41
00:02:37,247 --> 00:02:41,351
object that doesn't shadow it sees the change instantly. b

42
00:02:41,451 --> 00:02:43,961
now gives 75, while a still says 10.

43
00:02:47,033 --> 00:02:51,609
Practice question 1. Draw the prototype diagram for this Lamp code. List

44
00:02:51,709 --> 00:02:55,117
every box and every arrow. Pause the video and try it.

45
00:03:03,400 --> 00:03:08,023
Five boxes: the Lamp function, Lamp dot prototype holding watts 60, x

46
00:03:08,123 --> 00:03:13,021
holding its own color, Object dot prototype, and null. The arrows: Lamp's

47
00:03:13,121 --> 00:03:18,224
prototype goes to Lamp dot prototype, whose constructor points back to Lamp.

48
00:03:18,324 --> 00:03:22,948
x's proto goes to Lamp dot prototype. Then Lamp dot prototype's proto

49
00:03:23,048 --> 00:03:26,508
goes to Object dot prototype, and that goes to null.

50
00:03:30,600 --> 00:03:34,820
Practice question 2. What does this print? Pause the video and try it.

51
00:03:43,100 --> 00:03:46,678
First line: 40 and 75. l1 has its own watts of

52
00:03:46,778 --> 00:03:51,955
40, which shadows the prototype, and l2 sees the updated prototype

53
00:03:52,055 --> 00:03:56,833
value of 75. After deleting l1's own watts, lookup falls back

54
00:03:56,933 --> 00:04:00,592
to the prototype, so the second line prints 75.

55
00:04:04,666 --> 00:04:09,803
Recap. The function's prototype arrow goes to its prototype object, and constructor

56
00:04:09,903 --> 00:04:14,157
comes back. Each instance has a proto arrow to that prototype object,

57
00:04:14,257 --> 00:04:18,573
which links to Object dot prototype, then null. Own properties live in

58
00:04:18,673 --> 00:04:22,801
the instance box. And lookup simply walks the proto arrows until it

59
00:04:22,901 --> 00:04:26,334
finds the property, or reaches null and gives undefined.

